great thing about the Internet is it  disintermediate the message you know  people will now go to the website make  up their own minds they don't need a  professional leftist TV journalist  standing in front of the camera with a  microphone telling them what they ought  to think about something hi I'm Michael  Moynihan for recent TV and I'm here  today with M Hannon of southeast England  and Internet superstar yeah I think  you're a superstar at this point 2.5  million views of your great denunciation  of Gordon Brown in European Parliament  tell us a little bit was this a total  shock to you but out of nowhere you can  give us a general idea of what your  denunciation of Gordon Brown was well I  tried to say to him what I figured my  constituents would want to say to him if  they were there which is that he's  spending all their money and there's  nothing to show for it I was completely  flabbergasted by the way it took off I  still don't really understand why it may  have been that it was a three-minute  speech which is a kind of good YouTube  length it's about the average length of  a pop video maybe I don't know it may  simply have been that it was a surrogate  way of giving the guy a bit of a  slapping for all the people would have  liked to have done it themselves the  amazing thing is it shows that nobody  can predict what the public is going to  like great thing about the Internet is  it disintermediate the message you know  people will now go to the website make  up their own minds they don't need a  professional leftist TV journalist  standing in front of the camera with a  microphone telling them what they ought  to think about something this stuff  resonates obviously with the American  right and the American libertarian right  who are your political influences I've  seen you reference Ron Paul I believe  your breath reference Hayek and Friedman  on your blog I tell us a little about  all of those guys right always take the  rest as read you know in Ain Rand and  the area and in a British context Enoch  Powell you know he was as a somebody who  understood the importance of national  democracy who understood why you needed  to live in an independent country and  what that meant as well as being a free  market here in a small government  conservative but going a bit further  back some of the Patriot leaders who  founded the US on British political  principles although those principles of  subsequently lost in Britain but the  you know the The Jeffersons and the  atoms and the Washington's  the idea of a executive constrained by a  legislature of laws only being passed  with popular consent of taxes not being  raised except by elected legislators  those those were the ideals that have  done most to keep mankind prosperous and  happy and free you have a book called  the plan and it is a plan for what and  what is what are the sort of main sort  of tenants of the plan one way of  putting it is that it is an application  of Jeffersonian principles to UK  political conditions so we are pushing  for the ideal that decisions should be  taken as closely as possible to the  people that they affect decision makers  should be accountable through the ballot  box and the citizen should be as free as  possible from state coercion that sounds  very easy very banal when you translate  it into policy in a British context it  becomes incredibly radical it means we  would have elected sheriffs local sales  tax dispersal of power self financing  local authorities referendums and  initiative procedures shifting power  from the government to Parliament a lot  of things which you would take for  granted in North America and you know  which are often in a British context  attacked as my attempt to Americanized  Britain you know your own you're copying  all these things that states rights and  the sheriffs and so on  to which my answer is where did they get  those ideas from the Patriot leaders of  the 18th century you know they didn't  see themselves as radicals at the time  they saw themselves as conservatives in  their own minds when they took up arms  they were fighting to defend what they  had always assumed to be their  birthright as Freeborn British subjects  and the the innovators as they saw it  were those in the Hanoverian court who  were trying to suck power away from  Parliament's and into the executive so  we're in this strange situation where we  exported the tradition of British  freedom to this part of the world where  it thrived but in the mother country its  withered and so I I desperately want us  to bring back to repatriate our  revolution and bring back the principles  that have served to keep this country  free back to the place where they  originated  so currently you're trying to bring and  again again this is your critic saying  Americanization which is obviously a  dirty word in more of an American  direction where we have a government  that is desperately trying to bring us  more in a european direction so I  suspect the question that you're getting  quite a bit these days or questions  about the NHS what has your experience  been with the British system what would  you warn people away from this would you  tell them to embrace it what's your  what's your general take I would warn  people away because when you see a  friend about to make a mistake you tell  him you know you know you don't let him  walk into making the same mistake that  you made look this isn't just my opinion  you could you will always have anecdotal  stories about people having a wonderful  experience or a terrible experience what  you need to do is is try and be as  empirical as you can about this look at  the actual data you know would you  rather be ill in the u.s. system or in  the UK system and the answer if you look  at survival rates for cancer or stroke  or heart disease that is unquestionably  that you would be better off in the US  the that's that's just fact now some of  people watching this may have seen that  extraordinary film where Michael Moore  kind of goes waddling around saying ah  but you know mortality is better in the  UK life expectancy is long that's  probably true overall right life  expectancy is longer in the UK because  we have less teenage crime and this kind  of but the relevant question in terms of  health care is what is your life  expectancy from the moment that you are  diagnosed with out of these conditions  and the answer is it is far worse in the  UK than in not only than in the US but  then in other countries that have a kind  of mixed system for the most obvious  reason which is that the government runs  things badly it is no more competent at  running hospitals and it was at building  cars or operating airlines or installing  telephones the state is not a good  manager and this is something you guys  have dealt with for many years I mean I  always reference back to a reference the  yes minister episode where there is a  opening a hospital and they said we  don't need any patients we need a  hospital you would place to employ  people we don't actually have to treat  any what beers yeah and it's the perfect  Hospital because it's clean nobody dies  there because there are no patients you  know like so many things in that Yes  Minister series it was eerily prophetic  the NHS now employs 1.4 million staff of  whom a majority are administrators  the bureaucrats the managers outnumber  the doctors and nurses within that 1.4  million and that's what makes it such a  difficult system to get rid of because  you then have this huge block of people  who have a vested interest in the status  quo and that's why I would say to  Americans watching this think hard  before you start down this road don't  imagine that you can experimentally  bring in a government option and then if  it doesn't work out you can just reverse  it that isn't our experience it will  grow and grow it will start sucking in  more and more taxpayers money it becomes  politically impossible to deny demands  for greater resources and in the end it  squeezes to the very fringes any private  alternatives so that you end up in a  situation like in Britain where there is  private insurance for the very very  wealthy for a tiny number of people who  can afford to buy their way out of the  NHS even though they've already  obviously paid for it in their in their  taxes but it's such a tiny market that  there isn't really proper any any proper  competition within it and and therefore  90 plus percent of the population are  left dependent on a a state system which  you know contains good people and where  you can sometimes have good experiences  because there are kindly and dedicated  doctors and nurses in it but where  overall it is not nearly as good a  system as it would be if we had the kind  of consumer choice the opportunity for  address that you have in your system  David Cameron it is polling numbers now  or I mean overwhelmingly favoring the  Tories for the first time in a very long  time what do you think that the American  right can learn from David Cameron he is  sort of lunged towards the center in a  lot of ways what it what is well how are  these new Tories in your estimation any  as there any vestige of sort of  Thatcherism left in his people and is  there something that we as Americans or  the American right which is floundering  right now against Barack Obama can learn  from David Cameron I think there are  things the American right can learn it's  a gross oversimplification to say that  he has lunged towards the center in some  ways he has been much more radical than  any previous Conservative leader  including Margaret Thatcher not least on  this agenda of democratization and  decentralization of power he's gone  further on those things elected sheriffs  and local sales tax and so on than any  any previous  leader what would you learn I'd say this  look if you're a conservative it is very  easy to be negative because the world is  not the way we would like it heaven  knows that there are lots of things to  complain about and we complain you know  and in a way we're right to complain but  we we come across very easily as  curmudgeonly if all we do is complain  and the lesson you could take it from  from Cameron or you could take it from  Regan in a domestic context is that to  be a successful conservative you also  have to imbue your vision with a little  breath of warmth you have to convey a  sense of optimism that you don't just  criticize what's wrong although heaven  knows there is plenty to criticize you  also posit a vision of what could be  better you give the voters a sense that  there is a better tomorrow and that if  they vote for you there will be tangible  and immediate consequences that will  improve their lives and if you can do  that rather than being the angry old man  I find myself doing at some times you  know I was doing it when the Prime  Minister came to Brussels if you could  do that then people come with you Daniel  hem  thank you for him thank you very much  Michael Moynihan with recent TV and  thank you for watching

